<%#
====================================================================
FILE: src/views/dashboard.ejs
COURSE: CSE 340 - Web Backend Development (Week 5)
====================================================================

PLAIN ENGLISH: The private dashboard page. It shows the logged-in person's
               name and email address. Admins also see a link to the
               users page.
LOGIC: Rendered by showDashboard in src/controllers/users.js, which sends
       title, name, and email. The page only loads if the lock in the
       /dashboard route (requireLogin) finds a user in the session.
WHY WE NEED IT: Gives logged-in users a page of their own, and gives us a
                page to prove that route protection works.
LEARNING GAP: The data comes from req.session.user, saved at login. It is not
              looked up from the database again. This page never checks if
              someone is logged in by itself. The route does that before this
              page is ever rendered.
%>

<%# PLAIN ENGLISH: Add the shared top of the page (site header and menu).
    LOGIC: Pulls in src/views/partials/header.ejs.
    WHY WE NEED IT: Every page looks the same at the top without copying code.
    LEARNING GAP: The dash in the opening tag prints the partial's HTML as real
                  HTML. An equals sign would show the tags as plain text. %>
<%- include('partials/header') %>

<main>
    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: The page title at the top ("Dashboard").
        LOGIC: title comes from showDashboard (title: 'Dashboard').
        WHY WE NEED IT: Matches how every other page shows its title, and is
                        the "Dashboard" heading the Protected Routes activity asked for.
        LEARNING GAP: The equals sign tag prints text safely. If the text had
                      HTML in it, it would show as plain text, not run. %>
    <h1><%= title %></h1>

    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: A smaller heading over the person's info, then their name and email.
        LOGIC: name and email come from req.session.user, passed in by showDashboard.
        WHY WE NEED IT: Labels the account details clearly under the page title.
        LEARNING GAP: Changed in the W05 Assignment. This used to say "Dashboard,"
                      which repeated the h1 above (the h1 already prints "Dashboard"
                      from title), so the page showed the word twice. A different
                      label avoids the repeat and looks more professional.
                      If name or email showed blank, it would mean the names sent
                      from the controller didn't match the names used here. They
                      match the model's column names (name, email). %>
    <h2>Your Account Information</h2>
    <p>Name: <%= name %></p>
    <p>Email: <%= email %></p>

    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: Show the "View All Users" link only to admins.
        LOGIC: user comes from res.locals.user (set in server.js), not from
               showDashboard. If someone is logged in AND their role_name is
               'admin', the link is shown. Otherwise it is left out.
        WHY WE NEED IT: The W05 Assignment asks for a link from the dashboard
                        to the users page that only admins can see.
        LEARNING GAP: This only HIDES the link. The real protection is
                      requireRole('admin') on the /users route in routes.js.
                      A regular user who types /users is still stopped there.
                      "user &&" comes first because user is null when nobody
                      is logged in. On this page someone is always logged in
                      (requireLogin made sure), but keeping the same pattern
                      as the other admin links makes the code consistent. %>
    <% if (user && user.role_name === 'admin') { %>
        <p><a href="/users">View All Users</a></p>
    <% } %>
</main>

<%# PLAIN ENGLISH: Add the shared bottom of the page (site footer).
    LOGIC: Pulls in src/views/partials/footer.ejs.
    WHY WE NEED IT: Closes the page the same way as every other page.
    LEARNING GAP: The header partial opens tags that the footer partial closes,
                  so both are needed for a complete page. %>
<%- include('partials/footer') %>
